q-sunshine-pve for Proxmox VE 9
===============================

This is a headless Sunshine build for the private QEMU Display1 D-Bus route.
It does not modify a VM configuration, create a public D-Bus socket, open a
firewall port, or enable a service during installation.

Before enabling an instance, arrange for the target QEMU process to use the
Display1 D-Bus backend and a private, root-only D-Bus socket.  The supported
VirGL shape is equivalent to:

    -device virtio-vga-gl -display dbus,gl=on

Use a distinct socket and a distinct Sunshine port for every guest.  Never
point SUNSHINE_QEMU_DBUS_ADDRESS at a shared session/system bus.

1. Check the host and the packaged runtime:

    sudo q-sunshine-preflight --virgl

2. Create a root-only per-VM configuration:

    sudo install -d -o root -g root -m 0750 /etc/q-sunshine/instances.d
    sudo install -o root -g root -m 0600 \
      /usr/share/doc/q-sunshine-pve/example-instance.conf \
      /etc/q-sunshine/instances.d/100.conf
    sudoedit /etc/q-sunshine/instances.d/100.conf

3. Only after QEMU's private D-Bus endpoint exists, enable the matching
   service:

    sudo systemctl enable --now q-sunshine@100
    sudo systemctl status q-sunshine@100

The unit pins `capture=qemu_dbus`, but leaves encoder selection to Sunshine's
own live probe.  It deliberately does not treat an NVIDIA device node as proof
of NVENC.  Set `QSUNSHINE_ENCODER=nvenc` only after a real Sunshine probe has
verified that encoder; supported explicit values are `nvenc`, `quicksync`,
`amdvce`, `vaapi`, `vulkan`, and `software`.  `stream_audio` remains at
Sunshine's enabled default so the QEMU guest-audio backend is usable.  The
wrapper clears DISPLAY and Wayland environment variables.  Every
q-sunshine@VMID has isolated persistent pairing and configuration state below
/var/lib/q-sunshine/VMID/sunshine/; no state is shared between guests.

## Optional QSF companion

Clipboard, file transfer and a guest geometry request are not GameStream
messages.  They use the separate QSF companion.  It is intentionally not
enabled at package installation: first attach a private QEMU virtio-serial
server socket for the guest qsf-guest-agent, then add its path as
QSUNSHINE_QSF_AGENT_SOCKET to the same instance configuration.  The QEMU
process must create that socket before the broker starts.

Enable the local broker only after the guest agent and QEMU socket are ready:

    sudo systemctl enable --now q-sunshine-qsf-control@100
    sudo systemctl status q-sunshine-qsf-control@100

It creates a VM-scoped mode-0600 Unix control socket and token below
/run/q-sunshine/qsf-100/.  The token must never be copied to a client or
exposed through TCP.  q-sunshine-qsf-control maps the instance's private
Display1 D-Bus address to busctl for QEMU Console.SetUIInfo, so resize reaches
both the guest agent and QEMU without using a host session bus.

For a remote Qt client, create separate mTLS server/key/client-CA material per
VM, set the three QSUNSHINE_QSF_* TLS paths and deliberate bind/port in the
instance file, then enable the gateway:

    sudo systemctl enable --now q-sunshine-qsf-gateway@100
    sudo systemctl status q-sunshine-qsf-gateway@100

The gateway enforces TLS 1.3 and a client certificate.  Give the Qt client
only its client certificate/key and server CA; never give it the local broker
token.  Guest agent sources are installed in /usr/share/q-sunshine/guest/ and
the complete QSF protocol/CLI reference is /usr/share/doc/q-sunshine-pve/QSF.md.
Neither QSF unit edits a VM configuration, creates credentials, or opens a
listener until explicitly enabled.

For a user-selected client size, install the guest agent together with the
generation-bound VirGL display adapter source
`qsf_virgl_display_adapter.sh`. Its trusted guest-local apply helper must
restart/reconfigure the desktop and verify the actual scanout before it writes
`connection-profile-applied`; without that acknowledgement the broker safely
refuses to reconnect Moonlight. Set the host's tested encoder envelope in the
same instance file (`QSUNSHINE_QSF_HOST_MAX_*` and
`QSUNSHINE_QSF_HOST_ENCODER_CODECS`). Optionally configure loopback
`QSUNSHINE_QSF_SUNSHINE_SERVERINFO_URL` to intersect codec claims with the
live Sunshine probe. Full details and the exact v2 profile schema are in
`QSF_STREAM_NEGOTIATION.md` beside this README after installation.

Guest source examples are in /usr/share/q-sunshine/guest/.  Consult the
installed /usr/share/doc/q-sunshine-pve/SUNSHINE_QEMU_INTEGRATION.md for the
full Display1 protocol and security model.
